an10.12:6.1 starts: possessing five factors
an10.12:6.2 starts: and having removed five factors,
an10.12:6.3 starts: he is truly called a consummate one
an10.12:6.4 starts: in this Dhamma and discipline.
an3.29:4.1 starts: He does not possess such wealth,
an3.29:4.2 starts: nor does he do deeds of merit;
an3.29:4.3 starts: the blind man destitute of eyes
an3.29:4.4 starts: casts an unlucky throw in both respects.
an3.29:5.1 starts: The person described as one-eyed
an3.29:5.2 starts: is a hypocrite who seeks wealth,
an3.29:5.3 starts: sometimes righteously
an3.29:5.4 starts: and sometimes unrighteously.
an3.29:6.1 starts: Both by thievish cheatful acts
an3.29:6.2 starts: and by means of false speech
an3.29:6.3 starts: the man indulging in sensual pleasures
an3.29:6.4 starts: is skilled in amassing wealth.
an3.29:6.5 starts: Having gone from here to hell,
an3.29:6.6 starts: the one-eyed person is tormented.
an3.29:7.1 starts: One with two eyes is said to be
an3.29:7.2 starts: the best kind of person.
an3.29:7.3 starts: His wealth is acquired by his own exertion,
an3.29:7.4 starts: with goods righteously gained.
an3.29:9.1 starts: One should avoid from afar
an3.29:9.2 starts: the blind one and the one-eyed person,
an3.29:9.3 starts: but should befriend the one with two eyes,
an3.29:9.4 starts: the best kind of person.
an3.30:5.1 starts: Yet this person cannot grasp
an3.30:5.2 starts: anything from the talk,
an3.30:5.3 starts: at its beginning, middle, and end,
an3.30:5.4 starts: for he utterly lacks wisdom.
an3.30:9.1 starts: While sitting in his seat,
an3.30:9.2 starts: he comprehends the phrasing,
an3.30:9.3 starts: at the beginning, middle, and end
an3.30:9.4 starts: of the talk given by the bhikkhu.
an3.34:14.1 starts: Therefore a wise person should abandon
an3.34:14.2 starts: any deed born of greed, hatred, and delusion.
an3.34:14.3 starts: A bhikkhu, giving rise to knowledge,
an3.34:14.4 starts: should abandon all bad destinations.
an3.35:8.1 starts: He always sleeps well,
an3.35:8.2 starts: the brahmin who has attained nibbāna,
an3.35:8.3 starts: cooled off, without acquisitions,
an3.35:8.4 starts: not tainted by sensual pleasures.
an3.36:17.1 starts: “Its floor as well is made of iron
an3.36:17.2 starts: and heated till it glows with fire.
an3.36:17.3 starts: The range is a full hundred yojanas
an3.36:17.4 starts: which it ever covers pervasively.
an3.36:20.1 starts: But those good people here who,
an3.36:20.2 starts: when warned by the divine messengers,
an3.36:20.3 starts: never become heedless
an3.36:20.4 starts: in regard to the noble Dhamma;
an3.39:12.1 starts: Now I am incapable
an3.39:12.2 starts: of indulging in sensual pleasures.
an3.39:12.3 starts: Relying on the spiritual life,
an3.39:12.4 starts: never will I turn back.”
an3.40:4.1 starts: For one performing an evil deed
an3.40:4.2 starts: there is no place in the world called
an3.40:4.3 starts: The self within you knows, O person,
an3.40:4.4 starts: whether it is true or false.
an3.40:5.1 starts: Indeed, sir, you the witness
an3.40:5.2 starts: despise your good self;
an3.40:5.3 starts: you conceal the evil self
an3.40:5.4 starts: existing within yourself.
an3.48:3.1 starts: Just as the trees that grow
an3.48:3.2 starts: in dependence on a rocky mountain
an3.48:3.3 starts: in a vast forest wilderness
an3.48:3.4 starts: might become great “woodland lords,”
an3.48:4.1 starts: so, when the head of a family here
an3.48:4.2 starts: possesses faith and virtue,
an3.48:4.3 starts: his wife, children, and relatives
an3.48:4.4 starts: all grow in dependence upon him;
an3.48:4.5 starts: so too his friends, his family circle,
an3.48:4.6 starts: and those dependent on him.
an3.48:5.1 starts: Those possessed of discernment,
an3.48:5.2 starts: seeing that virtuous man’s good conduct,
an3.48:5.3 starts: his generosity and good deeds,
an3.48:5.4 starts: emulate his example.
